From 2accc2d07343c7f8a7c0e4bf27b023ea3a7fd521 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alan Carvalho de Assis <acassis@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 13:12:46 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] cdp1802: make stack pointer updates interrupt-safe

The CDP1802 changes R2 (SP) one byte at a time with PLO and PHI.  An
interrupt taken between the two writes enters the handler with X=2, and
a standard COSMAC handler saves the interrupted context by pushing
through R2 (RCA MPM-201A, Fig. 76).  The existing sequences can leave R2
up to 256 bytes *above* the live stack top for a few instructions: frame
allocation adds the low byte first, and loading SP from the frame
pointer, another register, a constant or memory writes the high byte
first.  A handler that interrupts there overwrites live stack frames.

Emit sequences whose every intermediate value is at or below the live
stack top, so that the handler's pushes land in free stack space:

- SP += V (addhi3/subhi3 with a constant): for 0 <= V < 256 keep the
  low-byte-first add; otherwise add the high byte first, then the low
  byte with carry.
- SP <- FP in the epilogue (new cdp1802_restore_sp_from_fp): SP <= FP
  there, so write the low byte first.
- SP <- register, constant or memory (direction unknown; memory loads
  come from non-local exits such as libgcc's SJLJ unwinder): first move
  SP one page below the new value, then write the low and the high byte.

Intermediate values are below the new SP by less than 512 bytes, so a
stack needs that much free space below its lowest SP (a "guard band"),
and no stack may live in page 0x00, where the arithmetic would wrap.
All other writes to R2 are single instructions (INC, DEC, STXD, LDA,
LDXA, IRX, OUT, RET, DIS).

